WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Every few months, a team from

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.500
the Australian Government
science agency, CSIRO,

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.940
and the Australian Bureau
of Meteorology

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.360
will bottle up air from here,
at the northern tip of Tasmania,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.400
and send it off to be archived.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.260
A few days ago, back on the
Australian mainland,

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.460
in a suburb of Melbourne,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.860
I interviewed one of the team
at the Air Archive

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.960
to explain how and why.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.400
- The first tank was taken on
the 26th of April, 1978.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.480
All up, we have close to 200 tanks
spanning almost 50 years.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.780
The pressure they get to is
around-about 900 PSI.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.080
They're 34 liter tanks,
which means they hold

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.080
around 2,000 liters of air
once they're completely full.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:41.400
- This place, Kennaook / Cape Grim,
has the cleanest air in the world...

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.860
when the wind's blowing
from the south-west,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.260
when that air has traveled
thousands of kilometers

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.360
across the Southern Ocean
without touching land.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.700
So that is a monitoring station,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.120
testing the levels of dozens of gases
and chemicals in the atmosphere.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.520
- When we say clean air
or baseline air,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.440
we mean that it has not had recent contact
with land or pollution sources.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.500
We know that it's very representative
of the mid-latitudes

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.020
of the Southern Hemisphere
background air.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.960
Most of the sources of the
major greenhouse gases

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.280
are in the northern hemisphere

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.680
because that's where the
major population centers are.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.520
It takes about a year to do
the inter-hemispheric transport,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.640
going across the equator
is much, much slower than

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.160
the mixing within a
hemisphere itself.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.100
We wait until the wind is coming
from the southwest direction,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.440
and normally we wait several hours.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.560
- Today, the wind is not blowing
in the right direction!

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.040
It's blowing in from the cities in
Tasmania and the Australian mainland,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.500
but the team here are going
to do a "practice fill"

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.080
of an archive cylinder, so they
can show you what would happen.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.920
And it's a really technical operation
that has to avoid contamination.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.580
- These cylinders are filled
cryogenically, under vacuum.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.740
When they're put into
the liquid nitrogen,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.560
the air naturally will be
sucked into the tanks

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.480
over a two- to three-hour period.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.240
- As any gas cools down,
it compresses.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.120
So initially the air rushes in
to fill the vacuum,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.360
but then it hits
liquid nitrogen-chilled stainless steel,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.360
so it cools and compresses
and more air rushes in,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.580
which cool and compresses,
and so on, and so on and so on.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.680
It's a really clever way to fill
a pressurized canister without pumps.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.780
- We then let it
heat up or warm up,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.540
and then we blast whatever
water is in there out.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.240
There is water in the tanks because
there's water in the atmosphere,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.500
and we don't do any drying.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.280
We don't want to use pumps or any
particular drying agent or method,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.190
as this can potentially
contaminate the tanks.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.980
- So I had an obvious question:
how do they know

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.040
that the air in the archive tanks
doesn't change over time,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.400
through some chemical reaction,
or osmosis through a faulty valve,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.720
or microbe contamination?

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.360
- We take measurements
of the archive regularly,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.940
maybe once a year we'll
"take a sniff" out of

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.920
each of the cylinders,
so to speak,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.280
to ensure trace gases are not
drifting or changing.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.500
There is a little bit of
moisture and water in there

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.380
and there have been concerns
where the microbes might be

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.260
changing the composition
for some trace gases.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.360
But from what we've
discovered so far,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.620
that's not really the case.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.560
- So they've got an archive.
What do they do with it?

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.660
Well, let's say someone
discovers that actually,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.940
there's a greenhouse gas or
some other contaminant

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.620
that we should have
been testing for,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.120
for the last 50 years or so.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.160
The researchers can
go to the archive,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.020
pull samples from the tanks,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.600
and use modern equipment
to test old air.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.900
Remember CFCs? They've been testing
for those on site here for years,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.900
but it turns out the replacements
also cause problems.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.980
- After the CFCs were the
interim replacements, HCFCs,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.620
and then the replacements
for those, HFCs.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.540
It's only recently
we've had the technology

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:34.740
to be able to measure those,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.740
to build up a history
back in time to 1978.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.200
That would not be possible
without the archive.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.940
Some of the measurements we do take
just 20-30ml of air.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.300
We can actually take measurements
down to parts per trillion.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.780
- There was one more thing that
Paul showed me back at Melbourne.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.360
The first tank taken here in 1978?

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.820
It's not the oldest one
in the archive.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.160
- Several years ago,
we did a media story,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.920
and we started to get inquiries
from the public saying,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.420
"we've got older air than you".

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.300
And it turns out there was
a lot of people out there

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.000
with old scuba tanks
sitting in their garages.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.060
And most scuba divers kept
a diary of when they dived,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.340
when and where the tank was filled.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.980
So people started to donate
their scuba tanks.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.380
So we now have air back to 1956.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.720
For some trace gases
that we're interested in,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.000
are perfectly fine to
take the measurements. 

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.620
- All right, I am... 

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.920
I'm standing somewhere windswept
and talking about infrastructure.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.146
It's like the fifth time!

00:04:32.769 --> 00:04:35.160
I'm amazed the microphone held up.
Thank you so much.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:35.940
- No worries!

